    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21633",
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/e5dbc74d2d1c59c058a4c405a4b11779d0121e8585314b50f42caf9a6f26d9c2.svg)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/f813d443cbacc9af617e0f0255cedb8111a67e75e578e14b49123b35b7c5ad7e.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/b8bf207792764f87b552ed02754ef6762bb9e75150c29eb9e169e91748b43b4c.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/e8d295df793ba9838b03fa7b291ce02ab986b36d9f1ac17619aad1d470a53f16.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/6216f2c9d498f49d4dc9f8c19fcaf70a58e1185c20208c71da084583246a8795.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/b60c66756e19af7aceeffbeeb4aef2e559baabb39c969fb04a7e266085ec4ee3.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/724a549f8279f8295d73775f54245179e2ca2f63ee3b039b90376615c7e1d825.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)",
